      "id": 1,
      "text": "Rothbury was settled in the 1860s and officially incorporated as a village in 1883.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Rothbury,_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Originally named Greenwood, the town was renamed Rothbury after its post office to avoid confusion with another Greenwood in Alcona County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Rothbury,_Michigan",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Nelson Green, a pioneer from Ohio, cleared land in 1865 and became Rothbury’s first permanent resident.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Rothbury,_Michigan",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Chicago & West Michigan Railroad’s arrival in the 1870s turned Rothbury into a rail hub that later joined CSX.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Rothbury,_Michigan",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "George and Mary Stouch opened the Double JJ Resort in 1937, and it now hosts the Electric Forest festival each June.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Rothbury,_Michigan",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Rothbury sits about four miles inland from Lake Michigan, giving it a milder climate than most inland Michigan towns.",
